Expenses product scope: `GET /api/v1/admin/spend/lines` includes `product_scope` (`status`, nullable `kind`, `reason`) from simpl_core's domain rule. Only explicit product-use tags admit a line; missing, mixed or contradictory tags stay outside reconciliation. Migration `20261014160000_expense_themes_and_product_scope.sql` seeds an exclusive operational theme catalog and six fixed use values, archives legacy context tags while preserving their assignments, and does not alter ROI allocations. Release simpl_core with `roi.spend_scope` first and refresh this service's locked core dependency before deployment. Deploy the dashboard and SimpL OS skill changes, then update the two finance roles through the reviewed role-seed diff without overwriting human edits. Existing bank lines require evidence-based classification before comparison becomes populated.

## Mailboxes on other providers (IMAP/SMTP, ENG-864)

A seller connects any mailbox that is not Gmail or Outlook with its address and password only. simpl_core does the work (`services/email/mailbox_connect.py`); these routes add the KMS cipher (`MAILBOX_KMS_KEY_NAME`), the channel activation every new email connection gets, and the notices.

- `GET /api/v1/integrations/mailbox` → `{connected, needsPassword, email, providerName}` (`needsPassword`: a login was refused, the password changed).
- `POST /api/v1/integrations/mailbox/connect` `{email, password}` and `PUT /api/v1/integrations/mailbox/password` `{password}` → `{status, email, providerName, integrationSlug, appPasswordUrl, helpUrl}`, always 200. `status`: `connected`, `use_integration` (Google or Microsoft: connect `integrationSlug` instead), `provider_pending` (no preset yet: the team is told on Discord once per domain and the seller is emailed when it is added; no password is sent or kept), `wrong_password`, `unavailable`, `rate_limited`, `invalid_email`, `not_connected`. 503 when `MAILBOX_KMS_KEY_NAME` is unset.
- `DELETE /api/v1/integrations/mailbox` erases the stored password and revokes the connection.
- Admin: `GET /api/v1/admin/mailbox-providers/requests?status=open|resolved|dismissed`, `GET …/catalog` (presets and redirect rules), `POST …/requests/{id}/resolve` `{kind: new_preset|extend_preset|extend_redirect, preset?, key?, addMxSuffixes?}` (409 when it would not make the domain match; the waiting sellers are emailed), `POST …/requests/{id}/dismiss` `{reason}`.
- The routes are gated like every integration (`_require_integration_access`): the `imap` row is beta until `is_beta` is cleared, so only admins can connect until then. Passwords are `SecretStr` and their length is checked in the handler, because a refused field would come back in the 422 body.

## Rules and conventions

- Keep FastAPI route handlers thin. Move reusable domain behavior to `simpl_core` or a service module.
- Use Pydantic schemas for request/response contracts.
- Keep environment variables documented in `README.md` and `.env.example` when adding new config.
- Use existing agent/service patterns before introducing a new orchestration style.
- Emails a user's SimpL agent sends go through the `email` channel plugin and `services/channels/agent_email.py`: a plain email in the agent's words, one link into SimpL, signed as the user's own agent persona. Pass `brain.notify_user` a `body` (and optionally `subject`, `route`); don't build HTML.
- A seller's outbound email signature is `GET`/`PUT /api/v1/app/email-signature` (`html`, `plain`). HTML is sanitized with `simpl_core.services.email.signature` and stored on `sales.user.email_signature_html`. Apply `migrations/20260907_user_email_signature.sql` before this route is deployed. The journey email flow appends it at send time; this route does not send mail.
- A seller's active hours, when the extension may send, are `outreach_prefs.active_hours` = `{"start": "HH:MM", "stop": "HH:MM", "timezone": "<IANA name>"}` (start inclusive, stop exclusive, start before stop). A missing field takes 07:00 / 19:00 / UTC, and an unset or invalid window runs on 07:00-19:00 UTC. `sales.resolve_active_hours(prefs jsonb[, at timestamptz])` resolves it: the extension task RPC gates on it, and `GET`/`PATCH /api/v1/app/preferences` return its answer as `active_hours_effective` (`start`, `stop`, `timezone`, `source` = `configured` | `unset` | `invalid`, `is_open_now`). `PATCH` merges `active_hours` one level deep, `null` resets it, and a value the resolver calls invalid, or a zone not spelled exactly as in IANA, is refused with 422. Read the window through the resolver (`services/active_hours.py`) instead of re-parsing the JSON. Review-answer follow-ups (`review_response_service._next_business_morning`) schedule at the start of the seller's window, 09:00 Europe/Rome without one, like simpl_core's retry triggers (simpl_core >= 0.8.2).
- Authenticate product routes with `auth.get_current_user`, which also refuses SimpL Challenge entrants; scope them with `simpl_api.guards`, whose `resolve_user_context` repeats that refusal against the database. Both are single places, so a new endpoint written against the ordinary dependencies inherits them. Do not reimplement either check per endpoint.
- Approval routes (journey-log `set-pending`, managed approve, Agent API draft approve and `bulk-approve`) and invite execution return the status the database settled on. simpl_core's dispatch authority rule can send an approved row back to `PENDING_APPROVAL` (`payload.authority.block_reason`) or cancel it, so callers must not assume `PENDING`; `bulk-approve` lists such rows under `withheld`, and invite execution answers 409. No approval path queues a calendar invite (`invite_call`, `send_calendar_invite_manually`); it is booked by `execute-invite-call` or closed by `confirm-manual-invite`. Journey-log `set-pending` and managed approve answer 409 (simpl_core `assert_queueable`), Agent API approve answers 409 `approve_on_card`, and `bulk-approve` leaves them awaiting approval and lists them under `approve_on_card`.
- Regenerating a pending draft is asynchronous (simpl_core `request_regenerate`; the outreach worker rewrites it). Seller: `POST /api/v1/app/journey-log/{id}/regenerate` `{additional_prompt?}` (other legacy fields are ignored) answers 202 `{kind: "queued", journey_log_id, request_id, trigger_id, requested_at, late_after_seconds: 900, regenerate_pending}`, where `regenerate_pending` is the draft's `payload.regenerate_pending` marker; only message drafts (`send_message`, `linkedin_connect`, `invite_call`) awaiting approval qualify (409 `invalid_transition`), 404 when the draft is not the caller's. Poll `GET /api/v1/app/journey-log/regenerate-jobs/{trigger_id}?request_id=` for `{status: queued|running|rewritten|replaced|review_request|no_result|failed|cancelled, trigger_id, request_id, journey_log_id, scope, requested_at, late, reason, reasoning, version_index, new_journey_log_id, new_journey_log_status}` (404 when not the caller's job). `POST /api/v1/app/journey-log/{id}/regenerate/cancel` answers `{cancelled, request_id, trigger_id}` once the regenerate failed or is late (15 minutes), else 409 `regenerate_not_cancellable`. Managed operators have the same three at `/api/v1/admin/managed/actions/{id}/regenerate`, `/api/v1/admin/managed/actions/regenerate-jobs/{trigger_id}` and `/{id}/regenerate/cancel`, acting for the draft's owner (`requested_by: admin:<id>`). The Agent API's `POST /api/agent/v1/drafts/{id}/regenerate` answers 202 `{journey_log_id, status: "queued", request_id, trigger_id, agent_instructions}` and `GET /api/agent/v1/drafts/regenerate-jobs/{trigger_id}` the same job shape. An approved Sales Domain `writer_regenerate` queues a forced rewrite per draft through simpl_core's command (`queued` / `skipped` / `failed` per row).
- While a regenerate is queued or running the draft is locked: every journey-log write (seller and Managed approve, edit, reject, reschedule, version switch, regenerate, review answers, invite execution, manual email/invite confirmation, Agent API approve/reject/update) answers 409 `{"detail": {"code": "regenerate_in_progress", "message": "..."}}` (the Agent API adds `error` and `agent_instructions`). Agent API `bulk-approve`/`bulk-reject` leave such drafts alone and list them under `regenerating`. A regenerate that failed unlocks the draft and keeps its marker (`state: failed`) for the card.
- Outreach previews are retired: `POST /api/v1/outreach/preview`, the `dry_run`/`preview` modes of `POST /api/v1/outreach/simulate` (`mode` is now required, `persist` or `traced`) and the customer chat's `build_outreach_preview` tool. simpl_api runs no outreach walk and no longer depends on simpl-outreach. `POST /api/v1/outreach/simulate` and `POST /api/v1/outreach/simulate-inbound` write a fake inbound message and its `message_received` trigger on any `ulc_id`, so they take `require_admin` (`sales.user.is_admin`: owner, admin, staff) and a customer's token gets 403, even on its own connection. `POST /api/v1/outreach/commit-preview` is removed (404); its only caller, the dashboard's outreach workbench, went in simpl_dashboard #99.
- Who a platform user is, for every admin view of one: `GET /api/v1/admin/users?ids=…&ids=…` (up to 200, `require_admin`) answers `users[]` in the order asked (unknown ids left out), each a `UserResponse` with `customer_name`, `icp_count`/`active_icp_count`, `on_vacation_now`, `auth_disabled` (from `sales.user_login_status`) and `loan` (the open `IdentityLoanOut` of a lent SimpL identity, else null). The customer detail's `users[]` and `GET /admin/customers/{id}/users` read the same query (`services/admin_user_reads.py`). An endpoint that lists people returns their ids with its own facts and lets the dashboard's user card read the rest here; do not copy user columns into another response.
- Who takes a team's meetings (simpl_core `TeamHandoffService`, `services/team_handoff.py`): each member's own hand-off (`sales.user.handoff_to_user_id`) and the team rules (`sales.responsibility.owner_user_id`), written as the team table. `GET /api/v1/app/team/handoff` (any member) returns `members[]` (`handoff_to_user_id`, resolved `taker_user_id`, `needs_colleague`), `rules[]`, `can_manage` and the viewer's `colleague_ids`. `PUT /members/{user_id}` `{handoff_to_user_id}` sets one's own (managers: anyone's); `POST /me/identity` `{handoff_to_user_id}` makes a `sales_user` a `sales_identity` handing every meeting to that colleague (other roles 403; the team snapshot back), for a salesperson whose calls a colleague takes; `POST /rules`, `PUT /rules/{key}` `{description, owner_user_id}` and `DELETE /rules/{key}` are for managers (owner, billing_admin, manager). Staff have the same at `/api/v1/admin/customers/{id}/handoff`. Refusals carry `detail: {code, message}`: `identity_needs_handoff` / `invalid_handoff` / `invalid_policy` (422), `not_found` (404). Every path that changes a member's role, activity or customer (`PATCH /app/team/role`, admin user PATCH, assign/remove, disable/enable, permanent delete) ends with `sync_team_handoff`.
- Customer notifications (`services/customer_notifications/`, migration `20261016140000_customer_notifications.sql`): what happens in a workspace, told to its team in the app and by email. Four kinds: `calls` (a call with a lead booked, from every `call.scheduled` ULC event `sales.record_meeting_booking` writes whatever the channel, then moved, dated, cancelled or a no-show, each told only to the people told about the booking), `replies` (a lead's first message in a conversation, never its text) `accounts` (an account that stopped sending for a reason the team can fix, from the Operations fleet: LinkedIn stopped because the extension runs on another person's LinkedIn (also told to that teammate), LinkedIn is signed out, or the account has no LinkedIn profile, each after an hour stopped; the extension offline a day with the autopilot on (`extension_offline`) or with LinkedIn steps waiting (`linkedin_offline`); the mailbox disconnected; migration `20261020170000_linkedin_sending_notifications.sql`) and `inbound` (`services/customer_notifications/inbound.py`, migration `20261017200000_inbound_notification.sql`: a person the inbound sources brought still waiting for an answer after the message's wait, once per signal, only while `services.inbound.people.derive_state` says `waiting`; told to the connection's owner and its receiver by default, a sales identity none; never what they wrote; listed in `GET /settings` only for an account in the inbound beta with an inbound process). `GET /api/v1/app/notifications` (`limit` ≤ 50, `before`) returns the viewer's `items[]` (`id`, `kind`, `title`, `summary`, `company_id` (a click opens it), `settings_section` (else the section that fixes the viewer's own stopped account), `created_at`, `read`), `unread` and `has_more`; `GET /unread` the count; `POST /read` `{ids?}` (null: all) answers the new count. `GET /settings` returns per kind its `label`, `description`, `noun`, `scopes` words (`team`, `own_self`, `own_other`, `own_hint_self`, `own_hint_other`), each active teammate's `scope` (`team` / `own` / `off`), `by_email` and `chosen` (false: their role's default for that kind), the `outside[]` addresses and `can_manage`. `PUT /settings/{kind}/members/{user_id}` `{scope, by_email}` sets one's own (managers: anyone's); `POST /settings/{kind}/outside` `{email}` (409 for a teammate's address) and `DELETE /settings/{kind}/outside/{id}` are for managers. An outside address hears every one of its kind by email only, and its email carries no link.
- Customer notification words and rules (dashboard Notifications page, platform admins; migration `20261017140000_notification_texts.sql`): `GET /api/v1/admin/customer-notifications` lists every message (`calls.booked`, `calls.link_booked`, `calls.after_call`, `calls.counted`, `calls.moved`, `calls.dated`, `calls.cancelled`, `calls.no_show`, `replies.first_reply`, `accounts.extension_offline`, `accounts.mailbox_disconnected`, `accounts.linkedin_wrong_account`, `accounts.linkedin_logged_out`, `accounts.linkedin_profile_missing`, `accounts.linkedin_offline`, `inbound.new_person`, `email.layout`) with `label`, `trigger`, `audience`, `fields[]` (`name`, `label`, `multiline`), `variables[]` (`name`, `label`: how the page writes it as `[label]` / `[se label: …]`, `description`; page copy is Italian), `delay` (`label`, `max_minutes`, null: right away), `rule` (`enabled`, `in_app`, `email`, `delay_minutes`, `email_batch_minutes`, `updated_by`, `updated_at`; null for the layout), `words.{it,en}` (`status` approved / draft / not_approved, `live`, `draft`: `id`, `fields`, `created_by`, `created_at`, `approved_by`, `approved_at`) and `sent` (last 7 days: `facts`, `people`, `last_at`); plus `languages`, `app_language` (`en`) and `digest_from`. `PUT .../{key}/words/{language}` `{fields}` saves a version to approve (400 `detail.fields` per field it cannot render); `POST .../{key}/words/{language}/approve` `{version_id}` (409 unless it is the latest); `DELETE .../{key}/words/{language}/draft`; `GET .../{key}/words/{language}/history`; `PUT .../{key}/rule`; `POST .../{key}/preview` `{language, fields, viewer: teammate|owner|outside}` answers `samples[]` (`source` real/example, `customer`, `created_at`, `title`, `summary`, `lines`, `email_subject`, `email_html`, `email_text`). Words are a small Mustache: `{{name}}`, `{{#name}}…{{/name}}`, `{{^name}}…{{/name}}`. Nothing goes out without approved words, and an email of a kind only with a layout approved with that kind's `about_` and `digest_` words.
- Role `sales_identity` (migration `20261010b_sales_identity_role.sql`): a sending account that never takes a meeting. Invited with `handoff_to_user_id` (admin `invite-user`, customer `invites`/`invites/batch`) or, in a customer setup, `handoff_to_email` naming another invitee; flow `invite_identity`. Without one it chooses on the onboarding `handoff` step: `GET`/`PATCH /api/v1/onboarding/session/{id}/handoff` (team members who take meetings, or the setup's other invitees). A salesperson joining a team (`invite_shared_icp`, role `sales_user`) whose calls a colleague takes turns their session into an identity's at the Accounts step: `POST /api/v1/onboarding/session/{id}/identity` sets the session's `role`, `flow_type` and `invite.role`/`invite.flow_type` (event `joined_as_identity`; 409 for any other session), so the calendar is no longer asked and the `handoff` step follows. A role change to `sales_identity` needs `handoff_to_user_id` unless the member already hands off. Customer-side invites are open to owner, billing_admin and manager, and to icp_manager on pay per call. They take `icp_ids` (customer `invites`/`invites/batch`, Settings asks it for every role but billing manager): the customer's live ICPs the invitee works on, stored as the invite's `icp_assignments` with each ICP's `company_count` (20 when 0) as daily limit; `[]` = none; 422 for an ICP that is not live on the customer; inviting an open invite again replaces them; omitted = the role's default (a salesperson or company identity gets every live ICP at finalize, others none).
- Inbound to onboarding: `POST /api/v1/admin/customer-setups/from-session/{session_id}` `{billing_options, name?, notify=true}` turns a request-access session into the owner's invited onboarding on the same login and session (setup + accepted owner invite, back at the first step with their answers kept). Returns `setup_id`, `invite_id`, `onboarding_url`, and `email_error` / `billing_error` when one of those did not go through. 409 for a session that is not an inbound request, already has a setup, belongs to a `sales.user`, or whose address has an open invite.
- `GET /api/v1/onboarding/session/{session_id}/booked-call` -> `{booked, start, end}`: the request-access call, read from Nicolò's calendar (the snapshot also carries it as `context.booked_call`). `GET /api/v1/admin/onboarding/followups/preview` -> `{enabled, in_send_window, notes, held, skipped}`: the follow-up emails that would go now (sent only with `ONBOARDING_FOLLOWUPS_ENABLED=true`), and those held back with `reason`; a note or hold names its `session_id`, or its `invite_id` for an unopened setup invite (kind `invite`). Follow-ups are at most two per stall (3 and 10 working days idle) and one a week per address, across sessions and invites. Each ends with a link to `/reminders/<token>` (simpl_sales), backed by `GET`/`POST /api/v1/public/reminders/{token}` (signed token, no login; `services/reminder_links.py`): `{language, first_name, company, snoozed_until, stopped}`; `POST {choice: two_weeks|one_month|stop, note}` snoozes every follow-up to that address until then and owes one check-back note after, or stops them for good (`followups_snoozed` / `followups_stopped` reason `asked` on the session, `snoozed` / `stopped` on an invite's ledger). This is the person's own choice, apart from a card the team puts on hold. `POST .../unsubscribe` is the one-click target of each note's `List-Unsubscribe` header. `GET /api/v1/admin/onboarding/pipeline` items carry `next_reminder` `{at, number, kind, email, due_since}` or null: the earliest follow-up planned for anyone in the card (`planned_followups`, the job's own candidates and rules; empty while the job is off); `due_since` is set once it is already due. With `?with_starts=true` (seconds slower: the Operations fleet and the first meetings; the dashboard reads the board first, then this) each live customer's card carries `start` `{phase: not_sending|sending|first_call|switched_off, first_send_at, last_send_at, sends_7d, first_meeting_at, problems: [{code, text, owner: simpl|customer}]}` and the answer `starts_read: true`: a customer still starting (`not_sending`, `sending`) stays on one card (its setup's, else its first session's) however long ago it went live, one no longer a customer is `switched_off` with nothing else read, and a card with no company shows the account's name. `GET /api/v1/admin/customer-setups[/{id}]` carries `payment` `{state: paid|payer_ready|waiting_for_payer|no_payer, paid_via, payer_email, payer_role, payer_progress, handed_over_by, handed_over_at, finalize_blocker}`; sessions add `pays`, `reminders_sent`, `last_reminder_at`, invites add `pays`, `opened_at`, `reminders_sent`, `last_reminder_at`. `POST .../finalize` answers 409 with `finalize_blocker` when the setup is neither paid nor has its payer past the card.
- Onboarding cards on hold (admin):
  - `POST /api/v1/admin/onboarding/pauses` `{session_id | customer_setup_id, reason: missing_feature|not_in_target|not_now|other, note, recontact_on?}` returns 201 with the pause. It answers:
    - 400 for a missing feature without a note, or a past date;
    - 409 when the card is already on hold, is finalized, or the session belongs to a setup (pause the setup).
  - `PATCH .../pauses/{id}` `{reason?, note?, recontact_on?}` changes an open pause; `recontact_on: null` clears the date, and a new date gets its own email.
  - `POST .../pauses/{id}/resume` brings the card back (409 once resumed). `GET .../pauses?session_id=|customer_setup_id=` lists a card's pauses, newest first.
  - A pause reads `{id, session_id, customer_setup_id, reason, note, recontact_on, recontact_due, paused_at, paused_by_email, resumed_at, resumed_by_email, resume_cause: staff|customer_returned|call_booked|colleague_request}`.
  - `GET /admin/onboarding/pipeline` items carry `pause` (null when not on hold). A card on hold keeps its `stage`, has `our_move: false` and no `next_reminder`.
  - Approve-for-activation, approve-agent-access, finalize-async, `customer-setups/from-session` and setup finalize answer 409 on a card on hold.
- Staff edits `PATCH /api/v1/admin/onboarding/sessions/{id}/profile|company|questionnaire|icp` change only the fields sent:
  - a blank LinkedIn clears it;
  - the questionnaire is merged one level deep into their answers;
  - each change is recorded as an `admin_edit` onboarding event and is not counted as the person's move.
- Inbound signals: the public API is `/api/ingestion/v1` (`simpl_ingestion`'s router, mounted by `api/v1/ingestion.py`). `POST /signals` takes canonical signals with a server key (`sk_agent_` holding `ingestion:write`; the channel is the `X-Ingestion-Connection-Id` header, else the customer's SDK channel `sdk:{customer_id}`; another customer's channel is a 404). `POST /signals/browser` takes them from a page with a browser key (`pk_inbound_`, `sales.ingestion_publishable_key`): only from an origin on the key's allowlist, at most `rate_limit_per_minute` new signals a minute on the key's channel (429 with `Retry-After`; the count is the channel's, so what its server keys sent in that minute counts too; default 120), never an `owner_hint`, and `consent` only as the page states it (none stays none); any website may call it (its own CORS layer, `BrowserSignalsCORS`). `POST /sources/{connection_ref}` takes a tool's webhook signed with the channel's secret. A batch is checked whole: a signal without a non-empty `external_id` is a 400 and nothing of the batch is kept. Accepted signals land in `integration.ingestion_inbox` (one row per channel and `external_id`), dated by the sender on `/signals` and `/sources` and on arrival on `/signals/browser` (a page cannot backdate one). With `INGESTION_DRAIN_ENABLED=true` each route drains the rows it accepted before answering (at most 20, 8 s, 3 s per lock wait) and an in-process job sweeps the rest every 2 minutes (`background_jobs/ingestion_inbox_processor.py`); off, nothing drains. `GET`/`PUT /api/ingestion/v1/playbook` (server key) reads and replaces the customer's inbound playbook, as `/api/v1/app/ingestion/playbook` (managers) and `/api/v1/admin/customers/{id}/ingestion/playbook` (platform admins) do; a `PUT` names the `revision` it replaces, a stale one is a 409. A customer's channels are `/api/v1/app/ingestion/channels` (list and detail for any seat; create, `PATCH` brief/fields/`active`, `server-keys` (`POST {label}` mints one more key, one per backend that sends, `ingestion:write` only, answered `{channel, key, api_key, connection_header}`; `/{id}/revoke` stops one and spares the others), `publishable-keys` (mint with `origin_allowlist`, `/{id}/rotate`, `/{id}/revoke`), `signing-secret`, `events` (the inbox, newest first), `POST /{id}/test-signal` (a signal marked `test` about `ada@example.com`, drained in the request: it shows in `events` as `ignored`/`test_signal` and writes nothing) for managers and SimpL admins); one channel per adapter and customer (a second, even created at the same moment, is a 409). A channel comes with no rule (2026-10-16): inbound stays off for the account (`has_inbound_process` false, every signal `no_matching_rule`) until a rule that acts is written in Settings > Inbound. Request bodies are closed: a field the API does not know is a 422. The rule owner picker reads the active seats from `GET /api/v1/app/team/handoff`. Rules are the customer's, `/api/v1/app/ingestion/rules` (`GET`, `POST`, `PUT /{id}`, `DELETE /{id}`: match kind/source/name/conditions (`$exists`, `$any_word` lists of whole words), action (`record_lead` places the person and wakes nobody), `owner_strategy` `existing_owner` or `fixed` with `owner_user_id`, an `agent_context` that overrides the channel brief, `seller_approval_required`, `wake_after_working_hours`, `playbook_section`, `move_to_stage` (a `mappings.lead_status` id in simpl_core's `MOVE_TO_STAGE_TARGETS`: the connection moves there forward only), `expects_reply` (whether somebody should answer the people the rule takes, Home v2's "Inbound waiting for an answer": null is the action's default, yes for `create_lead`; a write that leaves it out keeps it); `notify_only`, a `record_batch` kind, a stage or `expects_reply: true` on an `ignore` rule are a 422); `POST /api/v1/app/ingestion/rules/try` (managers) `{source, name, person?, company?, properties?}` says what SimpL would do with one of the account's events by its rules as they are, writing nothing (simpl_core `preview_signal` in a read-only transaction): `{action, rule_id, reason, owner{user_id, name, chosen_by}, takes_over[{user_id, name}], lead_id (only a person the account works), stage{target, target_name, current, current_name, outcome}, wakes_agent, wake_after_working_hours, approval_required, expects_reply, playbook_section}`, stage names as the owner's pipeline calls them; an event with no address or profile is about somebody new; `GET /api/v1/app/ingestion/sources` lists `{source, name, signals, signals_30d, last_seen_at}` by when the person acted, test signals left out, busiest of the last 30 days first; the admin dashboard has the same at `/api/v1/admin/customers/{id}/ingestion/rules` (read for staff, write for platform admins), plus `/sources` (the source/event pairs the inbox received, to route by source), `/playbook`, and `POST /requeue` (staff: `{inbox_ids?}` puts the customer's failed signals back for the drain, and, among named ids only, those no rule took (`no_matching_rule`, decided by today's rules), minus meeting signals a later one about the same event supersedes, and drains them in the request as far as its budget goes: `{requeued, superseded, drained}`). Settings > Inbound sources is a beta: every `/api/v1/app/ingestion/*` route but `GET /availability` refuses a customer outside `INBOUND_BETA_CUSTOMER_IDS` (SimpL staff pass), and `availability` tells the app whether to show the section (`available`) and whether inbound is live for the account (`has_inbound_process`, Raff's decision 7: an active inbound channel and an enabled rule that does not ignore; false outside the beta). SimpL Sales shows inbound in Home v2 only when it is true. SimpL's own funnel reports like a customer (`services/simpl_inbound_emitter.py`, env `SIMPL_INBOUND_INGESTION_URL`/`_KEY`/`SIMPL_INBOUND_CONNECTION_ID`), under source `request_access` (`meeting_booked`, `meeting_rescheduled`, `meeting_canceled`, and `system_email_sent` for the reminders to finish the request or book the call) or `onboarding` (`onboarding_invited`, `system_email_sent` for the onboarding-open note and the setup reminders, `onboarding_completed` from either finalize path, a customer setup's or a single session's, and `became_customer`: a customer Closed, paid with its account made (`sales_wbr.customer_closings`, the later of the two), sent by the nightly `activation_reconcile` for the owner's session of every customer closed in the last 7 days and dated when it closed, `services/simpl_won_customers.py`; SimpL's rule moves it to Closed). `system_email_sent` carries `subject`, `purpose` and `text`. The owner of a customer setup staff created is reported too, with `properties.setup_origin` `staff`. SimpL's calendars report through the same channel, for SimpL's calendars only (`channel_for_customer`): a booking made on an appointment page as source `calendar_booking` (`meeting_booked`, `meeting_rescheduled`, `meeting_canceled`, one per external guest, mapped by `simpl_ingestion.adapters.google_calendar`; `services/recall/booking_signals.py`, sent after the calendar sync commits and noted in the capture's `capture_metadata.booking_signal`). The lane applies such a meeting fact only to somebody an inbound source brought (request access, a form, a chat; simpl_core `meetings.came_through_an_inbound_source`, 2026-10-16); anybody else's booking is ignored there. A recorded call's attendee nobody knew is created by the recording linker, never sent (they were sent as `call_recording` on 2026-10-15/16). Guests who are active users of another SimpL customer are not sent. SimpL's rules for both sources: migration `20261015172500_simpl_meeting_fact_rules.sql`.
- Onboarding connects the mailbox and calendar on the accounts page (`/api/v1/onboarding/session/{id}/connections[/{toolkit}]`: `GET`, `POST {callbackUrl}` -> `redirectUrl`, `PUT {connectedAccountId}`; gmail, outlook, googlecalendar). The person has no `sales.user` row yet, so the account is parked in the session (simpl_core `onboarding_choices`) and the finalize (simpl_flow) attaches it with the hand-off.
- Onboarding sessions by login: `GET /api/v1/admin/onboarding/by-user/{user_id}` (`require_admin`) returns the full session envelopes (`AdminSessionSummary`, as `GET /admin/onboarding/sessions/{id}`) the login owns, most recently updated first, `[]` when none. The dashboard's support inbox uses it for people who write while still onboarding (no `sales.user` row yet). `/admin/onboarding/by-customer/{customer_id}` is the same through `onboarding.provision_link`.
- Display names for a batch of logins: `POST /api/v1/admin/reports/user-names` (`require_admin`, body `{"userIds": [...]}`, max 200) returns `{"names": {user_id: name}}`: `sales.user.full_name`, else the newest non-empty onboarding `profile_draft.full_name`, else the email's local part; logins with none are left out. The support inbox labels its thread list with it.
- The team's colleague hand-offs, for the sales Home (`services/handoffs.py`, where each stands in `services/handoff_status.py`): `GET /api/v1/app/handoffs` lists the caller's own (source or owner), open ones first plus those closed in the last 30 days; `?scope=team` lists the whole team's, for managers only (403 otherwise); `?company_id=` lists every hand-off at one company for any member, as its page shows the team's work there. Each item has both colleagues, the company and lead, the booking (`start`, `exact`, `emails`), the lead's quote, `steps[]` (`account_coordination_event`), `done` (the last lead-facing action the owner completed: the objective can stay `active` after the invite went out) and, while open, `waiting[]`: drafts to the lead awaiting approval on someone's connection (`approve`) and SimpL questions (`answer`) first, then `take_over` (requested, the owner's walk has proposed nothing), `information` (the source must answer) and `scheduled` sends. Read-only; `can_see_team` tells the app whether to offer the team view.
- The people the customer's inbound sources brought, for the sales Home v2 (`services/inbound/`, routes `api/v1/app/inbound.py`, inbound beta: 403 outside `INBOUND_BETA_CUSTOMER_IDS`; `enabled: false` without an inbound process): `GET /api/v1/app/inbound/people?scope=mine|team` is one row per person (an `inbound.lead.received` with `inbox_id` from a source that is not a meeting fact, on a connection working for the customer, a lent identity's included; never `ulc.origin`) from their most advanced connection, with owner, receiver (`coalesce(loan.handoff_to_user_id, user.handoff_to_user_id)`), first and last signal, `state` (`people.derive_state`, pure: hidden for Opted out / Non sales / dummy; customer, won, held, booked (Meeting Booked or an open booking), lost by stage; then, with a signal from `WAITING_FROM` on that is not replayed nor the team's chat message and whose rule expects a reply (simpl_core `reply_rule.rule_expects_reply`: never an `ignore` rule; `action_config.expects_reply` counts only when `true`; unset or null, `create_lead`): answered (a send, invite, seller message, team chat or onboarding invite, or a move to Pending or further after it), draft (a lead-facing journey row waiting since), waiting (with `agent`: working, later, chose_not_to_write, records_only); else parked (Monitoring), answered (Pending to Messaged or any contact), context), `can_ask`, and counts over everyone (`by_state`, `by_category`, `new` = the New inbound column) for the scope and for the viewer's own; at most 500 rows. The owner or the receiver of any of the customer's connections to the person sees them; managers (owner, billing admin, manager) see the team, anyone else asking for it gets their own. One statement, MATERIALIZED steps, 5 s timeout (~70 ms on SimpL, 2026-10-17). `GET .../people/{lead_id}/signals`: newest first (100), each with only the fields `signals.FIELDS` lists for its event (never person, context, consent, `replayed`, `setup_origin`, `onboarding_url`), a chat as one conversation of turns. `POST .../people/{lead_id}/ask-agent`: simpl_core `triggers.request_inbound_answer` (a copy of the signal's trigger, seller approval forced; 409 while one waits, a reply waits, or within 10 minutes of the last ask; writes `inbound.answer.requested`). `POST .../people/{lead_id}/not-in-target {note?}`: the person's connections in Monitoring to Messaged the viewer owns or receives (managers: all) move to Not In Target (`changed_by` `inbound_not_in_target:user:<id>`). `GET /api/v1/app/lead-timeline?include_inbound=true` adds the inbound lines (only `signal_name` and `origin`, a chat's run folded with `grouped_count`); without it the timeline is as before.
- A colleague's hand-off is passed on from one of its cards: `GET /api/v1/app/journey-log/{id}/handoff-redirect` returns the objective behind the card (`objective_id`, `responsibility_key`, `state`) and the colleagues it can go to (`targets[]`: `user_id`, `full_name`, `responsibility_key`, `description`). `POST` with `{to_user_id, reason?}` moves it (simpl_core `AccountCoordinationService.redirect`). The targets come from the team table, not from the caller. The old hand-off closes and its queued cards are cancelled, and the new owner is woken. A card that isn't the caller's, or has no `coordination_objective_id`, gets 404. A colleague the table does not allow gets 403, naming the allowed ones. A closed hand-off, or an open one already on the target's responsibility, gets 409. Repeating the same move replays it (`replayed: true`). Managed operators have the same pair at `/api/v1/admin/managed/actions/{id}/handoff-redirect`: the managed-scope check resolves the card's owner, the move is made as that owner, and the operator is recorded in `sales.audit_log` (`managed.action.handoff_redirect`).
- Reply confidence: the request and response shapes are the OpenAPI schema's (tag `app-reply-confidence`, served at `/openapi.json` when `EXPOSE_API_DOCS` is on). A reply goes out on its own only when every layer allows it: SimpL staff's layers in the dashboard (`sales_outreach.reply_confidence_settings`), then the seller's consent, `outreach_prefs.auto_approval` (`enabled`, `stop_on_lead_response`, `channels`) and its ICP overrides (`outreach_prefs.icp_overrides[<icp>].auto_approval`), edited through `PATCH /api/v1/app/preferences` (`channels` replaces the stored list whole; `[]` is stored as is). `GET /api/v1/app/reply-confidence/settings` reads simpl_core's `reply_confidence_authorization(user, NULL)`: `{all_paused, customer_enabled, email_enabled, linkedin_enabled, channels}`. The four flags are SimpL's layers (the channel flags fold in neither the switch nor the pause); `channels` is the function's verdict per channel through every layer, the seller's consent included, passed on unchanged: `{email: {allowed, block, block_kind}, linkedin_message: {allowed, block, block_kind}}`, `block` the first layer that refuses (`all_paused`, `customer_off`, `channel_off`, `seller_paused`) and `block_kind` its refinement (`staff`/`seller` for `channel_off`, `master_off`/`pause_on` for `seller_paused`), both null when allowed. Read whether a reply may go out on its own from `channels.<channel>.allowed`. 404 for a caller without a `sales.user` row; a malformed answer (a flag that is not a JSON boolean, a channel missing or of the wrong shape) is a 500 with an error log naming the user and the fields, never read as off. `GET /api/v1/app/journey-log/queued?limit&offset` lists the caller's queued automatic replies (`PENDING` with `journey_log.auto_send_at` set, simpl_core's one definition of a queued reply; owned through the row's own connection, `journey_log.ulc_id`, the rule return-to-review applies), by `scheduled_at`, with no time bound (`replies[]`: `id`, `scheduled_at`, `channel`, `lead_name`, `ulc_id`, `lead_id`; `total`); every listed reply can be returned. `POST /api/v1/app/journey-log/{id}/return-to-review` moves one back to `PENDING_APPROVAL` through simpl_core's `reply_confidence_return_to_review`, actor `user:<caller id>` (200 `{id, status}`, 404 not the caller's, 409 `detail.code = "not_returnable"`); the function records the move in `sales_outreach.outreach_events` (`reply_confidence.returned_to_review`), the only event a return writes. `set-cancelled` rejects `PENDING_APPROVAL` cards only: a queued reply is returned, never cancelled.
- The payload edits (`PATCH /api/v1/app/journey-log/{id}/payload`, `PATCH /api/v1/app/journey-log/{id}/payload/bulk`, Managed `PATCH /api/v1/admin/managed/actions/{id}/payload`) never write `payload.reply_confidence` or `payload.authority`: a `field` whose first dotted segment is one of them (`reply_confidence`, `reply_confidence.withdrawn`) answers 422 `{"detail": {"code": "payload_field_not_writable", "message"}}` and nothing in the batch is written. The API refuses before the edit reaches the database, and simpl_core's `JourneyLogRepository` refusal (`payload_field_not_writable:`) maps to the same 422, never a 404. The block is written by the outreach pipeline and simpl_core's reply-confidence functions, the authority by simpl_core's dispatch authority rule and the approval paths.
- "Propose other times" on an invite card: `POST /api/v1/app/journey-log/{id}/propose-times` with `{times: [{start, timezone}] (1-3), note?}` (`start` is ISO 8601 with its UTC offset).
- The app chat's outbox (`src/simpl_api/api/v1/app/conversation_outbox.py`, `services/conversation_outbox.py`): a seller writes to a lead on LinkedIn or by email from the Home. `GET /api/v1/app/conversations/outbox?ulc_id=` (the thread's connection; its owner or a teammate of the same customer) returns `senders`, every connection and channel the viewer may write from: their own connection to the lead, and a colleague's when they share a hand-off on that lead (an `account_coordination_objective` not cancelled, the viewer one participant, the connection the other's role connection), each `linkedin_message` and `email`, with `available`/`reason` (`not_connected`: no `sales.connection`; `no_mailbox`: no active Gmail/Outlook; `no_email_address`: `LeadContactRepository.find_best_contact` for the owner; `no_company`; or the dispatch authority rule's reason, asked of `sales_outreach.evaluate_dispatch_authority` before offering, e.g. `account_handed_off`) and one `is_default` (the thread's LinkedIn when it can send); and `messages`, the last 30 days of chat messages to the lead from any of its connections (`ulc_id, channel, sender_user_id, sender_name, message, subject, status, created_at, scheduled_at, executed_at` (once settled), `error`, `review_reason`, `can_withdraw`, `blockers` from `sales.user_dispatch_blockers` for a queued LinkedIn one). `POST /api/v1/app/conversations/outbox` `{ulc_id, channel, message, subject?}` queues a `send_message` journey_log on that connection (`message_type: response`, `payload.created_by = run_id_trigger = seller_chat`, `written_by`; email adds `recipient_email` and `subject`, the first line when none), linked to the hand-off objective for a colleague's connection, inserted `PENDING_APPROVAL` and approved by the writer through `JourneyLogRepository.set_pending_with_version` in one transaction so the authority trigger judges it like an approved draft; 201 with the settled status, 404 when the writer may not use the connection, 422 `{code: unavailable, reason}`. It goes out from the connection's owner: LinkedIn through their extension (three minutes after `scheduled_at`), email from their mailbox via simpl_flow's `gcp_journey_email_flow`, which the route starts at once (best effort; the deployment runs one flow at a time). The run is the connection's active one, else the standard journey's (`accb2e01-…`), else its latest. `POST /api/v1/app/conversations/outbox/{journey_log_id}/cancel` withdraws a queued one (writer or connection owner, row lock, `payload.cancellation.reason = withdrawn_by_writer`), 409 once a sender has it. `migrations/20261008c_outbox_read_grants.sql` grants django_simpl_user EXECUTE on the two functions; without it both reads fall back (savepoints) and the trigger alone judges the row.
  - **Checks:** the times are held to simpl_core's `proposed_times_problems` (future, offset matching the IANA zone, no duplicates); otherwise 422 `{code: invalid_times, problems}`. A card that isn't the caller's gets 404. A card that isn't an `invite_call` gets 409 `not_an_invite`. A card not awaiting approval gets 409 `not_pending`. A closed hand-off gets 409 `handoff_closed`.
  - **Effect:** the card is cancelled (`payload.cancellation.reason = seller_proposed_other_times`, with the times and the note), in one transaction with one of two effects:
    - `route: own_conversation`, for a card with no hand-off: a `human_review_resolved` re-entry on the card's connection, with `selected_option_id = propose_other_times`, `proposed_times` and `note`. simpl-outreach replies offering exactly those times.
    - `route: handoff_source`, for a card linked to an open objective: the owner's `need_information` with `proposed_times`, which wakes the colleague who started the hand-off, since they are the one talking to the lead.
  - **Replays and events:** repeating the call on a card already cancelled this way replays it (`replayed: true`) and writes nothing. A `user.action.proposed_other_times` outreach event is logged.
  - **Managed:** operators have the same route at `/api/v1/admin/managed/actions/{id}/propose-times`. It acts as the card's owner, records the operator in `cancellation.cancelled_by` (`admin:<id>`), and writes `sales.audit_log` (`managed.action.propose_times`).

- The funnel's ROI block is `POST /api/v1/app/reports/value` and `POST .../value/seat-request`. It is a staff beta: only SimpL staff (`is_admin`) get it, 403 for everyone else, and the response says `beta: true`. Setting `ROI_RELEASED_TO_CUSTOMERS=true` (also `1`/`yes`/`on`) in the API environment releases it to the customer's owners, billing admins and ICP managers; unset or any other value keeps it staff-only. The cost follows the model Stripe bills (a pay-per-call account line means pay per call; the database model only when Stripe cannot say). Staff can send `simulate_pay_per_call: true` to price a seat customer as if it paid per call (`simulated: true`, `viewer.can_simulate`); anyone else gets 403. The seat request is how ICP managers ask owners and billing admins for accounts through the governed in-app notification. `rolling` (7, 30, 90) is the window of the funnel metrics beside the block and `offset` (0-104) moves it back that many windows; the response's `period` is that window itself, not a pace: its leads and meetings, the new customers its meetings bring at the close rate, their value, what SimpL cost in it (seats: the subscription for its days at today's price; pay per call: the calls charged in it) and `roi`, with `has_earlier` to stop going back at the first lead, or at the window pay per call began in (`cost_counted_from` set, `roi` empty: the seats paid before are unknown). The monthly figures and the forecast are that window's pace. All arithmetic lives in `services/customer_value/model.py`. `sales.icp.deal_size` means the yearly value of one new customer won from that ICP, in the customer's billing currency; it is set with `PATCH /api/v1/app/icps/{icp_id}/deal-size`, and the ROI prefers it over the median of `sales.deal`. `GET /api/v1/app/reports/value/expected` is open to every user of a customer, released or not: only `meeting_to_deal`, `held_to_deal` (of the companies whose first call took place in the last 180 days, those sold on or after it: the Home's close rate; the customer's own from 10 calls held, `source: actual`, below that the other customers' pooled rate as it is, `source: benchmark`, never blended), `deal_value` (null until set or until 3 deals of their own), `cohort`, `icps` and `can_set_deal_value` (owner, billing admin, ICP manager, manager), for the simpl_sales Home's revenue estimate; no cost, ROI or seats. The close rate counts a deal only as one of the window's meetings sold on or after it (`company_funnel_facts.first_closed_at`), and its benchmark pools SimpL's own account (`PeerCustomer.internal`, Raff 2026-10-08) beside the paying peers; SimpL never enters a cohort, its size, the lead rates or the deal median.
- Billing runs without rollout switches: the pay-per-call cycle (record, three-day contest window, Stripe posting, invoices), the hourly Growth price transitions and the call-evidence moves into Negotiation always run. What a customer pays is decided per customer, by its billing model and its Stripe subscription. An account that must pay nothing (SimpL's own) is a pay-per-call customer in billing mode `comp`, a 100% coupon on its subscription: its calls are recorded, posted and invoiced like anyone's, at zero. The call-evidence job applies the evidence of the last 30 days it has not applied yet, at most 500 per run.
- Pay per call (`sales.customer_subscription.billing_model = 'pay_per_call'`): accounts are free and each company is charged once, when its first call that took place is attributable to SimpL (`sales_view.billable_calls`: the company moved into Negotiation, or into Closed, backed by evidence that the call took place (Recall recording, calendar acceptance, the outreach agent reading the post-call conversation, the seller's or an admin's confirmation); the customer claimed it in SimpL and wrote to it before the meeting), from `billing_model_since` on, at its ICP's `sales.icp.call_price_cents`: the EUR 70 base rate (`DEFAULT_CALL_PRICE_CENTS`, `services/call_pricing.py`; a stored price is never repriced), or a price the customer accepted in an order (an onboarding order prices the owner's ICPs from the pool calculator, see below); calls with no ICP use `default_call_price_cents`, EUR 70 when unset. For a customer whose adopted order is on terms 2.x (2.0.0 or the current 2.1.0), the order applied when the call was held decides that v2 rules apply, but the price comes from the order applied when the customer first worked the company (its claim: the customer's first `user_lead_connection` to it; their first order when the claim came earlier; `facts_snapshot.agreed_rate_at`), and the ledger bills only meetings the customer accepted (automatic acceptance at the deadline only when the order says so; from terms 2.2.0, accepted with the order or re-accepted before the call, a meeting nobody declined is accepted: `call_billing/meeting_eligibility.TACIT_TERMS_VERSION`). Every user of a pay-per-call customer who holds meetings (owner, manager, ICP manager, sales user) must connect their calendar to the notetaker: `GET /api/v1/integrations/meeting-recording/requirement` returns `{required, connected}` (the sales app keeps them on `/connect-calendar` until connected), only once the notetaker is open to them (the Recall integration's allowlist: SimpL's team until Recall is set up for customers; `services/recall/requirement.py`). SimpL's booking organizer (`BOOKING_ORGANIZER_EMAIL`) is never asked, and `POST /api/v1/integrations/meeting-recording/connect` answers 409 for it: its calendar holds every customer's page bookings (setup of the account: `docs/GOOGLE_ACCOUNTS_SETUP.md`). `sales.call_price_period` (view) lists each customer's price per ICP (`rate_rule` `icp`, `future_icp`, or `default` with `icp_id` NULL) with `valid_from` / `valid_to`, from the orders they adopted.
- Call rejections page (platform admins, `api/v1/admin/call_rejections.py`): `GET /api/v1/admin/call-rejections?state=open|closed|all&limit=` (`rows` newest first + `open_count`; each row: `subject` and `lines`, the team notice's own words, kind `declined`/`contested`/`no_show`, customer, who and why, company, booking source and date or the call's date, the charge, the billing exclusion, the support thread's message count and `awaiting_simpl`, closed by/note), `GET .../{id}` (row + thread `messages`), `POST .../{id}/messages {body}` (support thread + email to the customer's user; `email_sent`/`email_error`), `POST .../{id}/close {note}`, `POST .../{id}/reopen`.
