ng offers stay with the wizard), so the app asks its owner or billing admin on their next visit; `GET /api/v1/legal/agreements/{id}` returns the frozen text, order and SHA-256 hashes with `can_accept` (and `confirms_signature` for a new price of the onboarding targets that its signer approves with one click: `POST /api/v1/legal/agreements/{id}/approve` with `order_sha256`, applied at once; services/onboarding_price_change.py); `POST /api/v1/legal/agreements/{id}/accept` (`document_sha256`, `order_sha256`, `terms_version`, `signer_name`, `signer_capacity` (`Legale rappresentante pro tempore` or `Procuratore con poteri di firma dei contratti commerciali`, also a DB CHECK), `language`, `authority_declared`, `terms_accepted`, `specific_clauses_accepted` = `12, 15, 16, 18`, `variable_charges_authorized` matching the disclosed method) is open only to the setup owner or a customer `owner` / `billing_admin`; operators prepare, never sign. A newer offer supersedes an older one; a changed order needs a new acceptance (409). The onboarding snapshot carries `derived.agreement_required` and `derived.agreement_accepted`. Pay-per-call checkout links and subscriptions carry `simpl_legal_agreement_id` in their Stripe metadata, and `sales.payment_link.legal_agreement_id`.
- SimpL Growth: seats at an intro price for N months, then a regular price (defaults EUR 200 x 3 months, then EUR 350, per seat per month, VAT on top). Plan defaults: `GET /api/v1/admin/billing/plans`, `PUT /api/v1/admin/billing/plans/growth` (platform admins). Payment links, `POST /admin/customers/{id}/subscription/create` and onboarding `billing_options` take `product = "growth"` with `intro_price_eur`, `intro_months`, `regular_price_eur` (left-out terms take the defaults). A live Standard subscription moves to Growth in place with `POST /api/v1/admin/customers/{id}/subscription/growth` (`effective: next_renewal | now`, `dry_run`), its price changes with `POST .../subscription/growth/price`, and `GET /admin/customers/{id}/subscription` returns its state under `growth`. Stripe objects: `scripts/stripe_setup_growth.py`.
- Business numbers (platform admins) come from one engine (`services/billing/metrics.py` for today, `services/billing/history.py` for months, Europe/Rome), with every hover sentence in `services/billing/definitions.py` and returned by the API: MRR = expected monthly billing net of VAT and of discounts still running at the next renewal, of every subscription in force (active, past due, unpaid, canceling until it ends; paused collection in and flagged), plus Managed engagements billed by invoice (seats × monthly seat price) and each pay-per-call customer's calls a month × its call price (`services/billing/call_volume.py`: its calls held of the last 90 days ÷ 3, calls from before the move to pay per call included, when its first company taken is 90+ days old; otherwise the mean calls a month of the active customers and SimpL, an estimate: `mrr_calls_estimated_cents` / `calls_estimate_per_month` / `calls_estimate_customers` on the KPIs, `callsEstimated` / `callsEstimate` / `arr.estimated` on the Overview, `calls_per_month` / `calls_basis` on each row and Plans & prices customer; its own calls a lent SimpL identity brought are priced with the surcharge they bill, the charge's else the agreement in force at the booking, as `calls_surcharge_percent` on the row, the mean over the 90 days; an estimate stays at the list price); Cash in = paid invoices by payment date, net of VAT, minus refunds (a paid invoice brought in `amount_paid`, or `amount_due` when marked paid out of band; one settled by a credit note brought in nothing: `billing/coverage.invoice_cash_cents`, which the Payments tab's cash flow and transactions use too); To collect = open invoices, VAT included; churn per organization (a plan change is not churn) by `churnBasis` = `actual_end` (default: the month access ended) or `request` (the month they asked to cancel); SimpL's own account is out of every total. `POST /api/v1/admin/business/kpis` takes `months` or `historyFrom`/`historyTo` (yyyy-MM-dd) and `churnBasis`; each row carries `description`, `sub_label`/`sub_values` (e.g. "without SimpL"), `cell_hints` and `derived`; `companies_delivered` counts each account–company pair once, in the month `opportunity.icp_signal_opportunities_history` first assigned it (the history repeats an assignment on every pool refresh), with SimpL's own accounts as the `sub_values` difference; the BP actuals read it, with `internal_sdr`, `trial_churn`, `customers_pay_per_call`, `booked_calls` (without SimpL) and `churned_mrr`; past months follow invoices (by the period their lines bill), the current month today's subscriptions. `POST /admin/business/revenue` returns `mrr_cents`, `cash_in_cents`, `customers`, `new_customers`, `churned_customers`, `churn_rate` series. `POST /api/v1/admin/overview/kpis` takes `churnBasis` and returns `descriptions` with MRR/ARR, "of which paid for this period", active users (at least one SimpL action: a lead claimed in SimpL, a connection request, LinkedIn message or email sent, a pending action approved; the extension heartbeat is a separate "extension online"), engagement (users of customers with real product access with 5+ SimpL actions in the last 7 business days), new customers (first paid subscription, out-of-band invoice or Managed engagement in the month, month to date against the same days of last month) and churn. Deploy the dashboard with the API: the payload keys changed (`customer_mrr` is gone, the BP actuals read `total_mrr`).
- Plans & prices: `GET /api/v1/admin/billing/plans/overview` (platform admins) returns every plan on sale (`standard`, `growth`, `managed`, `pay_per_call`, plus `other` when a live subscription sits on a price the catalog does not know) with its list prices, its totals (customers, paying customers, billed seats, net MRR, by the Business definitions) and one row per live subscription on it (payment state, seats, seat price, MRR, renewal, discounts, `growth` state, `pay_per_call` model and ledger totals). A pay-per-call row also says what its next invoice carries: `open_calls` / `open_amount_cents` (billable charges not invoiced yet, `held` included, at their call prices), `call_discounts` (the subscription's discounts still running at the renewal) and `open_due_cents` (after them; 0 for a 100% gift, null with no live subscription). The `pay_per_call` plan sums those over the customers its totals count in `calls` (`calls`, `amount_cents`, `due_cents`); other plans have `calls: null`. `growth_defaults` carries the Growth terms on sale. It reads the snapshot `/admin/business/*` reads: refresh it with `POST /admin/business/refresh`. It does NOT change a plan or a price.
- Weekly sales review (WBR, platform admins, `api/v1/admin/wbr.py`, contract `audits/2026-10-13-sales-wbr/SPEC_B.md`): reads `GET /api/v1/admin/wbr/grid?customer_id=&from=&to=&user_ids=&tag=key:value&window=4` (two Mondays, at most 104 weeks; `user_ids` repeated), `/period?customer_id=&from=&to=&user_ids=&tag=&people=true` (any days, both included, at most 371, not starting after today: the numbers the page leads with, each counted once over the days from its records, never added up week by week; `{first_day, last_day, days, definitions_version, numbers}`, each number `key`/`label`/`unit`/`description`/`dimension`/`value`/`numerator`/`denominator`/`maturing`, the customer's then, with `people`, each selected identity's contacted/replied/engaged and each profile's or seller's meetings/starter calls and activations/outbound calls; manual values and closed weeks stay on the grid; since wbr-5 also the funnel's own numbers, which since 2026-10-16 are the grid's first section too (`funnel`, before `outbound`, `reply_steps`, `sales` and SimpL's `product`; the old `summary` is gone and each total is in one section): `companies_contacted`, `companies_replied`, `inbound_requests`, `calls_total`, `calls_outbound_recorded` / `_inbound_recorded` / `_total_recorded`, `activations_outbound_call` / `_inbound_call` / `_after_call` / `_without_call` (SimpL only), and its rates, `simpl_core`'s `STEP_RATIOS`, each a number over the one above it on the same days, with numerator and denominator; on the grid the same week by week, which can pass 100%; no records: `GET /cell` answers 422 for them; `response_rate`, `booking_rate` and `call_to_activation_rate` are no WBR number any more), `/cell?customer_id=&key=&week=&user_id=&channel=&step=` or `&from=&to=` instead of `week` for a period's number (`{total, records}`, at most 500, each `at`/`label`/`link`/`extra`; a rate cell lists its denominator, each record flagged for the numerator in `RATE_FLAGS`), `/health?customer_id=` (`{checks}`), `/identities?customer_id=`, `/origins?customer_id=` (the seller's); commands, each with `idempotency_key`: `POST /manual-entry` (count rows only: a rate is 422; a correction names the entry it `replaces_id`, else 409; the same key with another body 409), `POST /week-close` (a week that is over, once), `PUT /user-tag`, `POST /origin` (`confirmed`/`rejected` a stored origin, else 404). A command needs a person (a service principal gets 403). The grid is `simpl_core`'s `WbrGrid` and, on a manual cell, `note`/`evidence_url`/`entered_at`/`entered_by_name`. Totals are always the whole customer's; `user_ids`/`tag` choose the identity rows. Outbound contacted/replied/engaged, people and companies, are v2's people week by week read live (`sales_wbr.outbound_people_weekly`, 20261017160000: every week of the grid in one read, ~1 s on SimpL), the customer's and each identity's; meetings are `sales_wbr.meetings_booked`. There is no outbound cache: the nightly `wbr_weekly_fill` job (`WBR_FILL_ENABLED`) and `POST /refresh` are gone (0.4.153), and `/health` has no cache check. Sales counts the first sales calls (`sales_wbr.first_sales_calls`), one per meeting (both notetakers, two companies: one call; no company known: per person), in two lanes: `calls_outbound` (SimpL's outbound wrote to the company before anyone there asked through an inbound channel, and wrote to somebody there in the 60 days before the call), split by the notetaker's classification into `calls_outbound_demo` / `_discovery` / `_other` / `_unclassified`, and `calls_inbound` (every other sales call), split by the channel of the earliest SDK request before the call into `calls_inbound_request_access` / `_founder_chat` / `_onboarding` / `_other` / `_unknown` (splits are totals only); `calls_unattributed` must be 0. Calls not about sales, with clients the customer has (a live subscription, or active in the 60 days before the call; one gone longer is a new inbound call), its own company or vendors are in no count: `/health` lists them (`calls_listed_apart`). A call record lists every company of the call (else its person), its `classification`, `lane` and `channel`. Product rows, activations free/trial and first payments (the billing history's `paying_start`) appear on the platform's own customer only. Needs migrations 20261014100000, 20261014100100 and 20261014100200 before the API, 20261014235500 (`classification`) right after the API that reads it, and 20261015171500 (`first_sales_calls`) before the API that reads it; 20261015213000 (the 60-day rules, after 20261007b) needs no API change; 20261015223000 (`call_activations`) before the API that reads it; 20261015233000 (`outbound_people_between`) before the API that serves `/period`; 20261017160000 (`outbound_people_weekly`) before the API whose grid reads it. A customer after a call (`activations_*_call`) is a call in a lane (outbound + inbound) followed within 8 weeks by the customer's first activation, matched automatically (email, work domain, site, confirmed origin), each customer once on its earliest call; its records carry the call, `customer_id` (or `customer_setup_name`), `activated_at` and `matched_by`. Since wbr-6 (2026-10-18, Raff) a new customer (`activations`, free/trial, the lanes, `activations_without_call`) is Closed: it paid (its first activation) and its account exists, dated at the later of the two, a recorded call or not (`sales_wbr.customer_closings`, `call_closings`); the period adds `customers_to_activate` (Closed, nothing sent yet), `customers_paid_open` (paid, no account yet), `days_to_first_send` and `days_to_first_meeting` (medians in days), `customers_waiting_first_send` and `customers_waiting_meeting` (still customers only) and `expected_call_revenue` (eur: the pay-per-call bookings of the days accepted or awaiting acceptance, at their price, SimpL left out); a period number carries `count`, how many records a median or an amount is over. Needs 20261018090000 (after 20261006_meeting_booking_acceptance) before the API that reads it.
- SDR identities (SimpL's own LinkedIn sending accounts): `GET /api/v1/admin/sdr-identities/activity` (platform admins, `?refresh=true` skips the 10-minute cache) returns every user of SimpL's own customer with, per month from the first outreach event to today (Europe/Rome), `invites`, `accepted`, `messages`, `first_messages` (leads whose first successful LinkedIn message fell in the month), `first_messages_replied`, `first_contacts` (first invite or message per lead; a follow-on, `event_data.message_type = 'follow_on'`, is never a first message or contact, but counts in `messages`), `send_days` and `booked` (attributable first bookings from `sales_view.company_funnel_facts`, on the account whose lead moved, for SimpL or for a customer the account was lent to, split by that customer in `booked_by_customer`: `customer_id`, `customer_name`, `booked`), plus weekly `invites` / `first_messages`; `is_identity` is false for SimpL people writing from their own profile (`NOT_SDR_IDENTITY_EMAILS`), `partial_months` flags the first and current month. It only counts: what SimpL pays the identities is computed by the dashboard tool `/dashboard/tools/sdr-identities` (`services/sdr_identities.py`).
- ICP cards: `GET /api/v1/admin/customers/{id}/icp-snapshot?days=30` (admins, 1-90 Europe/Rome days ending today, `timezone` and `definitions` in the response) returns, per ICP of the customer: `pool_size` (companies matching it today), `proposal_days` / `claim_days` (dense per day: companies proposed to its users, distinct per day; connections created), `proposals` (distinct companies over the window), `claims` (deprecated sum) with `companies_claimed` / `leads_claimed` apart, all-time `meetings`, `calls_held`, `won` (= `closed`, deprecated) from `company_funnel_facts`, SimpL's only (attributable), plus the window's `meetings_in_window` / `calls_held_in_window` / `won_in_window` (a company counts under the ICP it was first claimed from), `customers` (companies in Customer today, never a win), `users` (assigned, with `is_active`, `daily_lead_limit`, `autopilot_enabled`), `setup` (enabled filters, industries and signals, roles, workflow name, verification, cross-ICP exclusion) and `call_pricing` (price, its source, the pool it was priced at, and the billable charges the ledger priced at it). One read for every ICP; `GET /admin/icps/{id}/stats` stays the single ICP's deep view.
- A signal is stored only if simpl_flow's matcher can run it: exactly one condition, either a post signal (below), an article signal (`collection` + `vector_name` + `embedded_text`; the 1024-d query vector is computed on save) or a SQL signal (`table`, `filters` as a list of SQL strings). Anything else (an empty filter, several conditions, an article without text) is a 422 `Invalid signal: ...` on the admin signal routes, the ICP duplicate route and the onboarding drafts `PUT` (which also computes article vectors, since finalize copies drafts as they are).
- ICP post signals (`/api/v1/admin/icps/{id}/signals`, admins) are one search over the LinkedIn posts index, run by simpl_flow: `{"mode": "all_match", "conditions": [{"collection": "sales_posts", "search_mode": "semantic" | "keyword" | "hybrid", "embedded_text": "<what the posts are about>", "similarity_threshold": 0.5, "keywords": ["series A", "seed round"], "keyword_match": "any" | "all", "date_range_days": 30}]}`. `semantic` reads the topic and threshold, `keyword` the keywords (posts containing any of them, or all: whole words, a phrase's words together and in order, case and accents ignored, no stemming), `hybrid` both (on topic and holding the keywords). Without `search_mode`, keywords plus a topic are `hybrid`, keywords alone `keyword`, else `semantic`. Create and update store only the keys of the mode (threshold strictly between 0 and 1, default 0.5; 1-20 keywords of at most 100 characters, a list or a comma-separated string, duplicates dropped; `keyword_match` default `any`; days 1-365, default 30) and drop the rest, `min_matches` and `embedding_vector` included: one matching post makes a company match, and simpl_flow embeds the text itself. A mode without its text or keywords, a value out of range or a SQL condition on `sales.post` is a 422; `POST .../signals/preview` answers 400 for a post signal (it previews articles only). The onboarding drafts `PUT` applies the same rule. Matches point at `sales.post` ids (`payload.matching_objects["sales.post"]`).
- ICP website signals search the companies' own sites (the landing index, run by simpl_flow) the same three ways: `{"mode": "all_match", "conditions": [{"collection": "landing_pages", "search_mode": "semantic" | "keyword" | "hybrid", "embedded_text": "<what the pages say>", "similarity_threshold": 0.5, "keywords": ["ISO 9001"], "keyword_match": "any" | "all"}]}`. Same rules as post signals (`services/search_signals.py`), but no `date_range_days`: pages carry no date. Matches point at page URLs and carry what to show: `payload.matching_objects["landing_pages"] = {"ids": [<url>], "evidence": [{"url", "title", "section", "page_kind", "snippet"}]}` (the export and the customer agent read the evidence; there is no table to look up). `POST .../signals/preview` answers 400 for website signals too; they have `POST .../signals/website-preview` (`{"filter_json", "scope": "icp" | "all", "max_results": 100}`, 422 for anything but one valid website condition): simpl_core's `open_landing_preview`, the search simpl_flow's matching runs, on the ICP's companies (default; 6 pages of 1,024 at once, no page started after 20 s, so a big ICP comes back `cut_short` with `companies_searched` of `icp_companies`) or the whole index (the best 1,024 sites), answering `companies_matched` and the best `max_results` with their page (`url`, `title`, `section`, `page_kind`, `snippet`, `score`). It needs `simpl-core[search]` (pyarrow), `MILVUS_URI`/`MILVUS_TOKEN` and `NEBIUS_API_KEY`.
- Message directions (`services/message_directions.py`, chat tool `preview_message_directions`, skill `message-directions`, customer and staff chats): the agent writes two to four ways of writing for an ICP (name, idea, `voice_guide` per layer, optional `outreach_strategy`), and SimpL's outreach writer writes each to the same two or three companies (shortlist items or `companies`) from those unsaved fields: research and `decide_first_contact` once per company, `writer_a` per direction (decide again when the strategy changes; `shortlist_writer.prepare_first_message` / `write_prepared(fields=)`). Shown as a canvas with a pick card; nothing saved. `run_prompt_setup(icp_id, direction=)` puts the picked fields up for approval and saves them with `source` `chat_message_direction`; `prompt_template_service.direction_picked_at` reads when that last happened, and until it has the shortlist auto-run adds companies without messages and shows directions first. Agent runs are recorded with `correlation_id` `message_directions:<customer>:<thread>`. Ledger `chat_coordinator_root` 11 declares the tool.
- Feature check and company questions (admins, `services/feature_check/`): `POST /api/v1/admin/feature-check` asks a yes/no question of an ICP's companies or a named list and streams P(yes) per company from Jev over its closest website and LinkedIn post passages (SSE; reads only). `/api/v1/admin/company-questions` keeps questions (`sales.mapping_feature`, source `dashboard`, a reworded one is a new version) and their answers (`sales.company_feature`), answers them for an ICP (SSE) or one company, and lists answers per company. An ICP's question can require an answer (`PUT /{id}/required`, `spec.required_answer` yes/no): simpl_flow then proposes only the ICP's companies whose current answers are all the required ones (`sales_view.icp_required_question`; no answer is not a pass). Required questions are asked ahead: every night at 22:00 UTC (`background_jobs/required_company_questions.py`) and at once when one becomes required or is reworded, only of companies the flow can propose that have data and no answer, best fit first, 10,000 a question a pass, each question only of the companies that passed the earlier ones. Shortlist candidates (`GET /api/v1/admin/customers/{id}/shortlist/candidates`, the studio's `shortlist_candidates`) leave out companies the ICP's verifier rejected or that answered a required question wrong, ask the required questions of the listed page's unanswered companies, start the opportunity verifier on the page's unchecked companies (simpl_flow `gcp_verify_shortlist_candidates_flow`, switching the ICP's verifier on), and give each remaining company `doubts` (required questions without an answer, a verifier still checking or skipped, and why).
- Calls with leads (`/api/v1/app/calls`): `POST /confirm` records whether a call took place (`{company_id | ulc_id, outcome: held | no_show | rescheduled, held_at}`); held moves the owner's leads at the company into the workflow's Negotiation stage, dated by the call (409 when the workflow has none). `GET /pending-confirmation[?scope=team]` lists the calls waiting on a word (the company in Meeting Booked, or moved on after the meeting with nobody saying how it went) (`state: awaiting`: over, counting as held at `confirms_at` unless someone says they did not take place, `doubted_by` the automatic evidence that says they did not; `state: undated`: no date, so never counted until one is given); `team` gives the customer's to owners, managers, billing admins and ICP managers, anyone else their own; `GET /unrecorded[?scope=team]` lists the calendar calls of the last 30 days the notetaker did not record (every bot failed) that the team organized, with somebody outside who did not decline and whom nothing else counts the call by (not an accepted invitee the team works, no company of theirs in Meeting Booked or with evidence within a day, nobody who had bought), each with its `capture_job_id`, `title`, `at`, `organizer_user_id`, `mine` and `people` (`email`, `response`, a `name` to offer); `POST /unrecorded/{capture_job_id}` `{answer: held | not_held | not_sales, people: [{email, full_name}]}` answers one once (409 after): held finds each person as the recording linker does (address, then name at the address's company) or adds them under the name given, gives the team's connection the call (else opens one for the organizer with its `lead_claimed` closed, so no first touch goes out) and writes `seller_confirmed` evidence tied to the event, which moves them into Negotiation and the WBR counts; not_sales labels the event `not_sales`; `GET /follow-on-drafts[?scope=team]` lists the messages SimpL wrote after a call (`message_type = follow_on`) still waiting for approval, with owner, lead, channel, subject and text, read-only (`team` on the same roles: a manager reads a seller's recap under the Home's "After the call"; only the owner approves it); `GET /billing-status` says, for a pay-per-call customer, whether each company's call is billable, why not, and where its charge stands. `GET /booked?from&to` lists the meetings SimpL booked for the caller's customer in the period (first move into Meeting Booked or Negotiation, attributable, from `sales_view.company_funnel_facts`, as the Sales WBR counts them), with `held_at` once the call took place; `calls_held`: the attributable companies whose first call took place in the period, by the date of the call (a pay-per-call customer's charged calls), with their ICP; `upcoming`: the attributable companies with an open booking dated after now and no call held yet, whatever the period (facts judged from a year back at least); `totals.booked`/`held`/`calls_held`/`upcoming`; whatever the billing model (the simpl_sales Home's Revenue reads it). When booked meetings are: `GET /meetings` gives each company's booking of the caller's customer (`meeting_at`, `to_confirm`, `status`, `outcome_due` two days after it with nothing recorded); `POST /meetings` `{company_id, meeting_at? | capture_job_id?}` records the date, a calendar event, or neither (not fixed yet) and never moves the company; `GET /meetings/calendar-events?company_id=` lists the caller's synced calendar events with that company's people. `GET /journey-log/{id}/details` carries `meeting_recap` on a "How did the meeting go?" card.
- LinkedIn voice messages: `POST /api/v1/app/extension/voice-transcripts` (raw audio body, max 3 MiB, container read from the bytes) returns `{transcript}` for the extension, through the simpl_sales proxy at `/api/v1/linkedin_data/voice_transcript`. Auth is `get_current_user` plus `require_extension_access`. Optional `?event_urn=` (the LinkedIn event) and header `x-simpl-extension-version` are only kept on the outcome row. Nothing is retried and the transcript is never stored: 413/415 refuse the audio before any paid call, 502 means the provider failed, 422 an empty transcript, and in every case the extension simply does not send that voice. Each request past the access check leaves one `app.operation_log` row (see Operation log). The cost goes through `simpl_tracker.clients.openai_audio.transcribe_audio` (`gpt-4o-mini-transcribe`, process `linkedin_voice_transcription`): tracker transport accounting does not see transcriptions, so do not call `audio.transcriptions.create` directly.
- Operation log (`app.operation_log`, `services/operation_log.py`): one row per outcome of a best-effort operation that can drop something unnoticed (`operation`, `outcome` succeeded/skipped/failed, `reason` code, `subject`, `source`, `client_version`, small flat `details`). simpl_api endpoints write their own rows with `record_operation` (source `simpl_api`); the extension reports what the backend never sees, e.g. a voice it could not download, through `POST /api/v1/app/extension/operation-logs` (source `extension`, `get_current_user` plus `require_extension_access`, 204), behind the simpl_sales proxy `/api/v1/extension/operation-logs`. Allowed operations are the `Operation` literal in `services/operation_log.py` (today `linkedin_voice_transcription`): a new use case adds its name there, no migration. Writing a row never raises and never fails the operation it describes; nothing reads the rows to decide anything. Migration `migrations/20261002_operation_log.sql` (backend-only, RLS on).
- Calls booked on a seller's calendar (2026-10-15, `services/booking_calendar`): every active `googlecalendar` and `outlook` connection gets a Composio trigger (Google "Event Sync", polled by Composio every 2 minutes; Outlook's change notification), created by the `booking_calendar_subscriptions` job (every 10 minutes, one instance) after reading the calendar 62 days ahead once; the trigger id is in `integration.user_connection.config.booking_trigger`, removed with the trigger when the connection is no longer active. Composio posts to `POST /api/v1/webhooks/composio` (signed with `COMPOSIO_WEBHOOK_SECRET`; 503 while unset, 401 on a bad signature, any other trigger slug acknowledged and ignored). A future, timed, one-off event with a lead of the calendar owner's customer on it (guest or organizer; colleagues never count) dates that company's booking (`source = calendar`, `created_by = calendar_sync:user:<owner>`, `call.scheduled`) and moves the lead's connection into Meeting Booked when it was before it (or Not Interested); the owner's own connection first, then one whose calls the owner takes (an identity). An event cancelled or deleted before its start cancels the booking naming it. `GET /api/v1/integrations/booking-calendar/requirement` answers `{required, connected, linkWithheld}`: `required` for call-taker roles created from 2026-10-16 (the app keeps them on the connect page until `connected`), `linkWithheld` for whoever saved a booking link without such a calendar (simpl-outreach no longer sends it). Onboarding: `PATCH /onboarding/session/{id}/extension-setup` answers 409 for the invite flows (`invite_full`, `invite_shared_icp`, `invite_new_icp`, `invite_manager`) until the session connected Google Calendar or Outlook or named another calendar (`provider_requests`, category `calendar`).
- Meetings and calls are counted once, the same everywhere: `sales_view.company_funnel_facts(customer_ids, since)` (a company's first booking, its first call that took place, its first close, and whether SimpL brought it).
