  - Migration `20261002_roi_vendor_quiz.sql` adds the `payer` mode and `roi.vendors.proposed_*`. It also seeds a reviewer and a proposal for every vendor on the Nov 2025 – Sep 2026 statement, plus the aliases that fold duplicates. The seed never overwrites a vendor that already has a reviewer.
- ROI time survey (token, not JWT): `src/simpl_api/api/v1/roi_survey.py`. `GET /roi/survey/bootstrap` and `GET /roi/survey/week` accept `week` (snapped to Monday) and `copy=true` to reuse the previous compiled week without writing it. Both answer only with allocations the person can still allocate onto: an initiative closed or merged after the fact is dropped from the payload, because it is no longer in their catalog and `POST /roi/survey/week` would refuse it and lock them out of the week. The bootstrap also answers `retro_pending` and `retro_quarters` (`[{id, label}]`): the one-shot retro for paid months before the weekly survey (`WEEKLY_SURVEY_START`, 2026-08-31) that no survey week reached. Who gets it comes from `roi.people_costs` and `roi.time_weeks`, not from a list of names, and only months from the month of the person's `from_month` count (a September hire never owes it); `label` names the months the window really covers (`Nov–Dic 2025`, `Lug–Ago 2026`). `POST /roi/survey/retro` takes `quarters: [{quarter, allocations}]`, needs every pending quarter, validates them all before the first write, fills only the weeks still empty and stamps `roi.people.retro_completed_at`, after which the retro is never offered again (a second save answers 400). `POST /roi/survey/proposals` keeps the proposer's `note` (returned to the admin queue as `note`) and refuses a sixth pending proposal from the same person.
- ROI time survey on the dashboard (migration `20261007_roi_survey_on_dashboard.sql`, needs simpl-core >= 0.8.70): a week is typed as `hours` per row, in any amount, and saved as each row's share of the total, normalised to exactly 100 (`normalize_to_hundred`, hundredths by largest remainder) in `roi.time_weeks.pct`, with the typed hours kept in `roi.time_weeks.hours`; the sum is never capped. `POST /roi/survey/week` and `/retro` still accept `pct` from older clients and normalise it the same way; a row may hold 0–168, and a week with no time is refused (400). `GET /roi/survey/week` and the bootstrap return `hours` on each allocation only when the whole week was typed in hours. Weeks owed (`survey_weeks_due`): every Monday from `WEEKLY_SURVEY_START`, or from the week of the person's `from_month` when later, to this week in Europe/Rome (or the last week of `to_month`), minus the weeks holding any split. The bootstrap answers `due_weeks` and `current_week`, and without `week` opens on the oldest week owed (this week once none is); `POST /roi/survey/week` answers the `due_weeks` left. `GET /roi/survey/me` (JWT, any logged-in user) matches the login to `roi.people.email` and answers `person`, `token`, `current_week`, `due_weeks`, `late_weeks` (owed weeks before this one) and `retro_pending`, or `person: null`; it never mints a token. `GET /admin/roi/survey/status` (`require_owner`) lists every roster person with `due_weeks`, `late_weeks`, `last_week`, `retro_pending` and `has_login`. Whoever is not an owner sees only their own time: the bootstrap carries `summary` (`time_summary`): `weeks_filled`, `hours_typed`, `months` and `lines` `[{id, name, overall, by_month}]`, each the average share of the person's weeks (projects inside their line, merged rows on their destination), in percent of time, never money. `PUT /admin/roi/people/{person}` takes `email` (lower-cased, unique; 400 when it already belongs to someone else) and `GET /admin/roi/people` returns it.
- ROI proposal decisions: `POST /admin/roi/proposals/{id}/decide` takes `action` = `publish` | `reject` | `recategorize` and answers with `decided_by` / `decided_at`, both stored on the row. Publishing under a different `parent_id` re-derives `category`, `lag_profile`, `roi_ranked` and `sort_order` from that line via `simpl_core.roi.catalog.child_defaults` — the same helper the create path uses, so the two cannot drift.
- ROI catalog merge (`require_platform_admin`): `GET /admin/roi/initiatives/{id}/time` lists who filed time on a row (`person`, `weeks`, `pct_avg`). `POST /admin/roi/initiatives/{id}/merge` takes `into` and an optional `redistribute: [{person, allocations: [{initiative_id, share}]}]`; everyone not named follows the merge onto `into`, and each named person's time on the row is split by `share` (each in (0, 100], summing to 100). The whole request is validated before the first write: a person with no time on the row, a person named twice, a destination that is closed, merged or still awaiting review, or `into` being one of the row's own projects answers 400 and changes nothing. A line's open projects move under `into` (re-deriving category, lag, ranking and sort order via `child_defaults`), and merging a line that has projects into a project is refused. The answer lists `redistributed` and `moved_projects`.
- ROI timeline (`require_owner`: it shows wages by month, down to one person's month): `GET /admin/roi/timeline` returns `months` (`YYYY-MM`, continuous) and, for every line and project that is not transit or merged, `people` and `vendors` as `{month: euros}` (only months that carry something; a merged row's months sit on its destination). `people_without_hours`, `seats_without_hours` and `untagged` are the month's cost no row carries. A payment whose decision covers several months puts one share a month on the rows, up to the current month; `deferred` is the bank's payment minus those shares (positive in the payment month, negative after) and `deferred_after` what falls after the last month. Per month, rows plus those four equal wages (`roi.people_costs`) plus non-transit spend. `payroll_through` (`YYYY-MM` or null) is the last month an employee's wage has been paid for (`services/roi.payroll_through`: a contractor's invoice or meal vouchers alone do not count); wages leave the bank in the middle of the next month or later, so the Business Plan takes no ROI month after it. Needs simpl-core >= 0.8.75 and migration `20261008_roi_amortization`.
- ROI cost breakdown (`require_owner`, wages by person): `GET /admin/roi/cost-breakdown` reads the same months the timeline does, by person and by vendor instead of by line, for the Business Plan's actuals (`services/roi_costs.py`). It returns `months`, `people` (`[{person, months: {month: euros}}]` from `roi.people_costs`) and `vendors` (`[{key, name, area, months}]`, the key from `simpl_core.roi.vendors.canonical_vendor`, name and area from `roi.vendors` when the vendor is in the catalog, largest spend first). Spend is spread and filtered as the timeline does: a decided line over the months it covers up to the current month, an undecided one in its own month, shares on transit rows (`PAYROLL`, `NON-SPESA`) left out. Per month, people plus vendors equal the timeline's rows plus `people_without_hours`, `seats_without_hours` and `untagged`. It carries the timeline's `payroll_through` too.
- ROI month close (`require_owner`, for the BP Composer's "Chiusura del mese"): `GET /admin/roi/close-status?month=YYYY-MM` (`services/roi_close.py`) reads the payroll the timeline reads and answers `month`, `payroll_through`, `employees` (`[{person, paid}]`: every roster person who is not a contractor and works some day of the month, from `from_month` to `to_month`; `paid` when the bank paid them a wage for that month), `employees_expected`, `employees_paid` and `unrecognised_wages` (how many people-cost lines no payroll rule reads, booked from the month's first day on). No amounts: wages stay in the timeline and the cost breakdown.
- ROI summary (`require_owner`: cash and burn of the whole company): `GET /admin/roi/summary` (`services/roi_summary.py`) returns `as_of`, `payroll_through`, `cash` (`{amount, through, bank, drift}`: `amount` is what the open EUR accounts hold in `financials.ledger`, inflows minus outflows and fees, `through` the ledger's last day; `bank` is `{amount, as_of}`, the sum of each open account's latest `financials.account_balance` row, written nightly by simpl_flow's Revolut sync with `source='revolut_api'`, null until every open account has one; `drift` is ledger minus bank. Every runway and the projection start from the bank figure when there is one, from the ledger otherwise), `months` (the 12 closed months up to `payroll_through` and before the current month, each `{month, people, vendors, one_off, collected, net_burn}`), `burn` (`{window, net_avg, last, split_last, split_projected, unclassified_last}`: the last three closed months and their mean net burn; `split_*` are `{month, people, vendors, collected, gross, net}` for the last closed month and for the first whole projected month; `unclassified_last` is the last month's outflow with no decision yet, counted as vendors), `vendors_top` (`{month, rows: [{vendor, amount, share}], others}`: the eight recurring vendors of the last closed month), `runway`, `projection`, `people_pace`, `one_offs` (`[{vendor, month, amount}]` over the 12 shown months) and `warnings` (`[{code, severity, ...}]`, codes `cash_stale`, `payroll_behind`, `unrecognised_wages`, `paid_not_on_roster`, `person_not_projected`, `one_off_leak`, `burn_jump`; the dashboard words them). `runway` keeps `months`, `runs_out` (cash over `net_avg`) and `at_last_month` for the old dashboard, and adds `last_pace` (`{months, runs_out}`: cash over the last month's net burn), `projected` and `without_planned` (`{months, runs_out, cash_until, horizon_reached}`: from the projection, with and without the roster rows whose first day is after today; `without_planned` is null when there is none). `projection` (`services/roi_projection.py`, null before the first paid wage) walks the cash from the month after `payroll_through` (never past the current month: the 13th salary can close December the day it is paid) until it goes under zero or 36 months: `{from, basis, months: [{month, status: open|projected, people, vendors, collected, net_burn, cash_end, starting}], without_planned_cash: [{month, cash_end}] | null, not_projected: [{person, reason}]}`. People in a projected month are every roster row active in it, priced as the payroll prices a paid month (`simpl_core` `monthly_payroll_cost`: gross from RAL, employer charges, the hiring relief while it lasts, the first month prorated on the first day, plus their benefits a month, meaned over their last three paid months or, with fewer, the seasoned team's mean), contractors at the mean of their last three invoices, people without RAL at the mean of their last three whole paid months; a row with a RAL but no first day and no payslip is left out (`reason: no_start_date`, or `no_ral`, `no_invoices`, `invalid_dates` when the last month precedes the first day). What the bank already paid for a month after the payroll (benefits booked ahead such as the welfare spread over the year, a contractor's invoice) is netted off that month's people. Vendors and collected go on at the mean of the burn window; an `open` month (before or equal to the current one) only charges what the bank does not hold yet, where the current month's booked vendors are the spread shares `spend_by_vendor_month` gives it. `people_pace` is one row per roster person from that month on: `{person, status: active|joining|planned, planned, basis: ral_model|last_paid_month|contractor_invoices, basis_month, monthly, regime_month (the first whole month `monthly` is priced for), from_month, to_month (dates), relief_until, reason}`; the sum of `monthly` is the team at regime, not a projected month's `people`, which prorates and phases people in. People and vendors are the cost breakdown's numbers; `vendors` leaves out the lines whose current decision has `payment_frequency = one_time` (the vendor set to "Una tantum"), which go to `one_off` and stay out of net burn; `collected` is the collected revenue below. `net_burn = people + vendors - collected`. The roster itself (`GET /admin/roi/people`) answers the same pricing per person: `status` (null for someone who left before the projection starts, or before the first paid wage), `planned` (a first day after today, whatever the payroll), `projected_monthly`, `inps_relief_monthly`, `inps_relief_months`, and `monthly_cassa` is the last month with a wage (also in `PUT /admin/roi/people/{person}`'s answer). It reads the bank lines like the summary does, so it costs a full payroll rebuild per call.
- ROI collected revenue (`require_platform_admin`): `GET /admin/roi/revenues/collected` reads Stripe at most every five minutes per API instance (`services/roi_collected.py`, a `RefreshCache`: the last rows are served while a refresh runs or fails; while the first read has not succeeded the route, and `GET /admin/roi/summary` with it, answer 503) and returns every payment in and refund out as `{month (YYYY-MM-01), day, kind: payment|refund, number, customer, amount}`, euros net of VAT by each invoice's own share (`total_excluding_tax / total`), oldest first. A paid invoice counts in the month it was paid: `amount_paid` on Stripe, or `amount_due` when marked paid out of band (a bank transfer). Open, void, or settled by a credit note before payment: nothing, since a credit note alone moves no money. Refunds count negative in the month they left: Stripe refunds (`succeeded`, charge tied to an invoice) and credit notes' `out_of_band_amount`. SimpL's own customer (`billing.catalog.INTERNAL_CUSTOMER_NAMES`, matched on the invoice's `customer_name`) is left out with its refunds and credit notes, as the KPIs' cash in leaves it out. The Business Plan's revenue actuals read it.
- Bank statements: `src/simpl_api/api/v1/admin/bank_statements.py` (logic in `services/bank_statements.py`, tables from `migrations/20261001_bank_statement_import.sql`). The dashboard parses the bank's CSV and `POST /admin/finance/bank-statements` (`require_owner`, **dry run unless `dry_run: false`**) replaces every `financials.ledger` row of `account_id` dated inside `period_start`–`period_end` (or since the beginning when `from_account_opening`), plus any row of the account carrying one of the statement's transaction ids, with the statement's rows — one transaction. Replaced rows are kept whole in `financials.bank_statement_import.replaced_rows`; an outflow category on a replaced row is carried to the new row with the same id, and so is the sync's `external_leg_id` when the replaced transaction was a single leg (the sync and webhook upsert on it); the statement is refused (422) when its balances do not move by exactly its rows (inflows − outflows − fees; from zero with `from_account_opening`); month-end balances go to `financials.account_balance` (`source='statement'`). `PUT /admin/finance/accounts/{id}/balances` types balances (`source='manual'`; `balance: null` removes a typed one, never a statement's). `GET /admin/finance/accounts` and `GET /admin/finance/balances?since=` are `require_platform_admin`. Cash should be summed from balances: the ledger was never complete since each account opened.
- Bank ledger by month (`require_owner`, for the BP Composer's "Chiusura del mese"): `GET /admin/finance/ledger-months?from=YYYY-MM` (`services/bank_ledger.py`, default and floor `2026-09`, earlier months 422: the ledger is complete only from September 2026) reads `financials.ledger`, never `finance.cash_month`. It answers `from`, `through` (the current month in Europe/Rome), `accounts` (each account open in `from` or later: `id`, `bank`, `currency`, `operational`, `opened_at`, `closed_at`, `ledger_through` = last row's `occurred_at`, and `months` up to the current month or the month it closed: `{month, balance, pending_rows, other_currency_balances, movements: [{id (source_transaction_id), day (Europe/Rome), direction, amount, fee_amount, description}]}`) and `syncs` (the last successful `financials.sync_run` per `source`: `started_at`, `finished_at`, `window_to`, `latest_txn_completed_at`). Only completed rows count (`NOT provisional` and state `completed` or null); the others are counted in `pending_rows`. Movements and `balance` are in the account's currency: `balance` is every completed row since the account opened up to the month's last day (inflows minus fees, outflows plus fees); rows in another currency (Revolut's USD pocket) only move `other_currency_balances`. The dashboard classifies the movements itself; whether a month is complete is its call from `ledger_through` and `syncs`.
- Reply confidence routes: shapes in the OpenAPI schema (tag `app-reply-confidence`); routes in `src/simpl_api/api/v1/app/reply_confidence.py`, services in `src/simpl_api/services/reply_confidence/`.
- Agent code: `src/simpl_api/agents`
- Internal conventions: `.agent/INTERNAL.md`

ROI category catalog: `GET /admin/roi/categories` reads live database rows;
`POST /admin/roi/categories` accepts `label` and `allows_time` (default true),
requires platform admin, and returns the category id. Repeated or concurrent
submissions of the same normalized name reuse the category without changing it.
New categories default to operative lag and unranked ROI. Survey and vendor quiz
bootstraps include `categories`; the survey includes only `allows_time` categories.
Apply `20261010112322_roi_live_categories.sql` after the Asset migration and before
deploying core/API/dashboard: it replaces fixed category checks with foreign keys,
preserves existing categories and allows service-role access only.
